  "worked_solution": "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe distance between the line through the center of mass $(4,1)$ and the line through the point $(2,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(2-4)^{2}+(-1-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & =3.2+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =3.2+64 \\\\  & =67.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $67.2\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}.$",
